WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.468
Lesson four.

00:00:01.468 --> 00:00:05.325
How do you combine two neural networks with different shapes?

00:00:05.325 --> 00:00:08.252
NEAT's answer is the innovation number.

00:00:08.852 --> 00:00:10.480
Here are two parents.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:17.833
Because every gene has an innovation number, we can line their genes up by number, even though the networks look different.

00:00:17.833 --> 00:00:20.097
Parent A is the fitter one.

00:00:20.697 --> 00:00:23.838
Genes that both parents have are called matching.

00:00:23.838 --> 00:00:29.789
A gene that one parent has in the middle of its list, where the other has a gap, is called disjoint.

00:00:29.789 --> 00:00:34.607
And a gene that comes after the other parent's last gene is called excess.

00:00:35.207 --> 00:00:39.307
The child takes each matching gene from either parent at random.

00:00:39.307 --> 00:00:43.065
The disjoint and excess genes come from the fitter parent.

00:00:43.065 --> 00:00:51.337
No matter how different the two parents look, the child is always a sensible network, because every gene keeps its place in history.

00:00:51.937 --> 00:00:54.914
Crossover is one part of a generation.

00:00:54.914 --> 00:00:57.631
Let's put the whole loop together.

00:00:57.631 --> 00:01:03.496
First, evaluate: every one of the 150 genomes flies, and gets a fitness score.

00:01:04.096 --> 00:01:08.781
Then, speciate: sort the genomes into species of similar networks.

00:01:08.781 --> 00:01:11.867
That's the topic of the next lesson.

00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:22.694
Then, select: inside each species, the best twenty percent become parents, and the two very best are copied unchanged into the next generation.

00:01:23.294 --> 00:01:29.290
Finally, reproduce: crossover and mutation fill the population back up to 150.

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:33.494
Then the loop starts again, a few hundred times.
